Answer: Validation ensures that only acceptable values are assigned to private fields. Example:

```java
public void setBalance(long balance) {
if (balance >= 0) {
this.balance = balance;
```

} else {

```java
this.balance = 0;
}
}
```

A negative balance is rejected and a valid/default value is stored instead.

Answer: The setter can check whether the supplied value is null before assigning it.

```java
public void setAccountNumber(String accountNumber) {
if (accountNumber != null) {
this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
```

} else {

```java
this.accountNumber = "0000";
}
}
```

Answer: The setter can validate the password before assigning it.

```java
public void setPassword(String password) {
if (password != null) {
this.password = password;
```

} else {

```java
this.password = "1111";
}
}
```

This prevents null from being stored as the intended password value.

Answer: Yes. A method can accept multiple parameters and assign values to multiple fields. Example:

```java
public void setData(String accountNumber,
String password,
long balance) {
this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
this.password = password;
this.balance = balance;
}
```

A method can accept multiple parameters, so separate setter methods are not compulsory for every field.

Answer: Multiple setters update fields individually:

```java
setAccountNumber(...);
setPassword(...);
setBalance(...);
```

A single setter can receive several values together:

```java
setData(accountNumber, password, balance);
```

Both approaches can be used depending on how the data needs to be assigned.

Answer: Each getter returns one value. For example:

```java
public String getAccountNumber() {
return accountNumber;
}
public String getPassword() {
return password;
}
public long getBalance() {
return balance;
}
```

This also allows each getter to have the return type required by its field.

Answer: An instance variable is a variable declared inside a class but outside its methods. Example:

```java
class Student {
int id;
String name;
String email;
long phone;
}
```

Here, id, name, email, and phone are instance variables.

